Foundation
1. Cloud Computing
 Cloud Computing is the delivery of computing services such as servers, storage, databases, networking, software, and analytics over the internet instead of using local computers or physical servers. It allows users to access resources on demand, pay only for what they use, and scale resources easily.

2. Virtualization
 Virtualization is the technology that allows multiple virtual machines (VMs) to run on a single physical server. Each VM has its own operating system and resources, making hardware utilization more efficient while reducing cost.

3. AWS Basics
 Amazon Web Services (AWS) is a cloud computing platform provided by Amazon. It offers more than 200 cloud services, including computing, storage, databases, networking, security, AI, and DevOps tools to build and manage applications.

4. AWS Account Setup
 AWS Account Setup is the process of creating an AWS account by registering with an email address, verifying identity, adding payment information, and enabling security features such as Multi-Factor Authentication (MFA). It is the first step to access AWS services.

5. AWS CLI Configuration
 AWS CLI (Command Line Interface) Configuration allows users to interact with AWS services using terminal commands instead of the AWS Management Console. It is configured using Access Key ID, Secret Access Key, Region, and Output Format.

6. IAM (Identity and Access Management)
 IAM is a security service that controls who can access AWS resources and what actions they are allowed to perform. It manages users, groups, roles, and policies to provide secure access to cloud resources.


# Compute Services

7. EC2 (Elastic Compute Cloud)
 Amazon EC2 is a virtual server service that allows users to launch, manage, and scale virtual machines in the cloud. It is commonly used to host websites, applications, and backend services.

8. AMI (Amazon Machine Image)
 An AMI is a pre-configured template used to launch EC2 instances. It contains the operating system, software, configurations, and required applications, making server deployment faster and more consistent.

9. Elastic Beanstalk
 Elastic Beanstalk is a Platform as a Service (PaaS) that automatically deploys, manages, and scales web applications. Developers only upload their code, while AWS handles the infrastructure, load balancing, monitoring, and scaling.

10. AWS Lambda
 AWS Lambda is a serverless computing service that allows users to run code without managing servers. It automatically executes functions in response to events and charges only for the execution time used.

