# Database Services
15. RDS (Relational Database Service)
Amazon RDS is a managed relational database service that supports databases such as MySQL, PostgreSQL, MariaDB, Oracle, and SQL Server. AWS automatically handles backups, updates, and maintenance.

16. DynamoDB
Amazon DynamoDB is a fully managed NoSQL database service designed for high performance and low latency. It automatically scales and is commonly used for real-time applications, gaming, IoT, and mobile apps.



# Part 2: Networking + Security + Load Balancing + CDN
Networking

17. VPC (Virtual Private Cloud)
A Virtual Private Cloud (VPC) is a logically isolated virtual network in AWS where you can launch and manage AWS resources securely. It gives you complete control over IP addresses, subnets, routing, and network security.

18. VPC Creation
VPC Creation is the process of setting up a private network in AWS by defining an IP address range (CIDR block), creating subnets, configuring route tables, internet gateways, and security settings. It is the foundation of AWS networking.

19. IP Addressing
An IP Address (Internet Protocol Address) is a unique numerical address assigned to every device connected to a network. It helps identify and communicate with devices over the internet or a private network.
Types:
Public IP
Private IP
IPv4
IPv6

20. Subnets
A subnet is a smaller network created from a VPC. It divides the VPC into multiple sections, allowing better organization, security, and resource management.
Types:
Public Subnet
Private Subnet

21. Internet Gateway (IGW)
An Internet Gateway (IGW) is a networking component that connects a VPC to the internet. It enables resources in public subnets to send and receive internet traffic.

22. NAT Gateway
A NAT (Network Address Translation) Gateway allows instances in a private subnet to access the internet for updates or downloads while preventing inbound internet connections. This improves security by keeping private servers hidden.

23. Route Tables & Routing
A Route Table is a set of rules that determines where network traffic should be directed. It controls communication between subnets, internet gateways, NAT gateways, VPNs, and other networks.

24. Security Groups
A Security Group is a virtual firewall attached to AWS resources such as EC2 instances. It controls inbound and outbound traffic by allowing only specific ports, protocols, and IP addresses.
Example:
Allow HTTP (Port 80)
Allow HTTPS (Port 443)
Allow SSH (Port 22)

25. Network ACL (NACL)
A Network Access Control List (NACL) is an optional security layer that controls inbound and outbound traffic at the subnet level. Unlike Security Groups, NACLs support both Allow and Deny rules.

26. Ports
Ports are logical communication endpoints used by applications and services to send and receive network traffic. Different services use different port numbers.
Examples:
22 → SSH
80 → HTTP
443 → HTTPS
3306 → MySQL

27. Route 53 (DNS)
Definition:
Amazon Route 53 is AWS's managed Domain Name System (DNS) service. It translates domain names into IP addresses and routes users to the appropriate AWS resources.

28. DNS Working
DNS (Domain Name System) converts human-readable domain names (like example.com) into machine-readable IP addresses. This allows browsers to locate and connect to the correct server.

29. Custom Domain
A Custom Domain is a unique website address purchased from a domain registrar and linked to cloud resources. It provides a professional identity for websites and applications.
Example:
www.mywebsite.com
api.mywebsite.com
Security

30. Firewall Rules
Firewall Rules define which network traffic is allowed or blocked. They protect cloud resources from unauthorized access by filtering traffic based on IP addresses, ports, and protocols.

31. SSL/TLS Certificates & Encryption
SSL (Secure Sockets Layer) and TLS (Transport Layer Security) are security protocols that encrypt data transmitted between users and servers. They ensure secure communication and enable HTTPS for websites.


