# CDN & Content Delivery
36. CloudFront (CDN)

Definition:
Amazon CloudFront is AWS's Content Delivery Network (CDN) service. It stores copies of website content at edge locations around the world and delivers it to users from the nearest location, reducing latency and improving performance.

37. CDN Concepts

Definition:
A Content Delivery Network (CDN) is a globally distributed network of servers that caches and delivers static content such as images, videos, CSS, JavaScript, and HTML from locations closest to users. This improves website speed and reduces server load.

# Part 3: Containers + Serverless + Infrastructure as Code (IaC)
Containers & Orchestration
38. Docker Basics

Definition:
Docker is a containerization platform that packages an application along with its dependencies, libraries, and runtime into a lightweight container. This ensures the application runs consistently across different environments.
> Benefits:
Portable
Lightweight
Fast Deployment
Consistent Environment
39. Manage Containers

Definition:
Managing containers involves creating, starting, stopping, restarting, monitoring, and deleting Docker containers. It ensures applications run efficiently and can be updated or scaled when needed.

Common Docker Commands:
docker run
docker start
docker stop
docker restart
docker ps
docker logs
40. ECR (Elastic Container Registry)

Definition:
Amazon ECR is a fully managed Docker container registry provided by AWS. It is used to securely store, manage, and retrieve Docker images that can later be deployed to ECS or EKS.

Purpose:
Store Docker Images
Version Control Images
Secure Image Repository

41. ECS (Elastic Container Service)
Definition:
Amazon ECS is a fully managed container orchestration service that runs and manages Docker containers without requiring users to install or maintain their own orchestration platform.

Purpose:
Deploy Containers
Manage Containers
Scale Applications
Monitor Container Health
42. EKS (Elastic Kubernetes Service)

Definition:
Amazon EKS is a managed Kubernetes service that simplifies deploying, managing, and scaling containerized applications using Kubernetes. AWS manages the Kubernetes control plane while users manage their applications.

Purpose:
Kubernetes Management
High Availability
Container Orchestration
Automatic Scaling
Serverless & Integration
43. API Gateway

Definition:
Amazon API Gateway is a fully managed service used to create, publish, secure, monitor, and manage APIs. It acts as an entry point for applications to communicate with backend services such as Lambda, ECS, or EC2.

Purpose:
API Management
Authentication
Rate Limiting
Monitoring
44. SQS (Simple Queue Service)

Definition:
Amazon SQS is a fully managed message queue service that enables applications to exchange messages asynchronously. It helps decouple application components and improves reliability.

Purpose:
Queue Messages
Reduce System Dependency
Improve Reliability
Process Background Tasks

45. SNS (Simple Notification Service)
Definition:
Amazon SNS is a fully managed messaging service used to send notifications to multiple subscribers through email, SMS, mobile push notifications, or other AWS services.

Purpose:
Send Alerts
Broadcast Messages
Event Notifications
Application Integration
Infrastructure as Code (IaC)

46. CloudFormation
Definition:
AWS CloudFormation is an Infrastructure as Code (IaC) service that allows users to create and manage AWS resources using YAML or JSON templates instead of manually configuring them.

Purpose:
Automate Infrastructure
Consistent Deployment
Easy Resource Management

47. CloudFormation IaC
Definition:
CloudFormation IaC refers to defining the entire cloud infrastructure as code using CloudFormation templates. The templates describe AWS resources, their configurations, and dependencies, allowing repeatable and automated deployments.

48. Terraform

Definition:
Terraform is an open-source Infrastructure as Code (IaC) tool developed by HashiCorp. It allows users to provision and manage infrastructure across multiple cloud providers such as AWS, Azure, and Google Cloud using configuration files.

Benefits:

Multi-Cloud Support
Version Control
Automation
Reusable Infrastructure
49. Infrastructure as Code (IaC) Concepts

Definition:
Infrastructure as Code (IaC) is the practice of managing and provisioning infrastructure through configuration files instead of manual setup. It enables automation, consistency, faster deployments, and version control for infrastructure.
